# Independent Review

Completed non-trivial work is verified **independently** — by a fresh
auditor subagent, never the doer reviewing itself. How that auditor
behaves (its independence, verifying rather than trusting, what it judges,
and its verdict and bounded loop) lives in `roles/auditor.mdc`; the
organizer launches it (see `roles/organizer.mdc`). This rule covers
**when** review happens and **where** the verdict goes.

## When to review (gated)

Review is **required** for **planned tasks** — work carried out under an
agreed plan (the plan-based pathway).

**Skip** review for one-off trivial edits — typos, comments, or tiny
mechanical changes that carry no behavioral risk. Lone-agent (one-shot)
changes are not planned, so they are not audited; the lone agent
self-verifies instead (see `roles/lone-agent.mdc`).

That carve-out turns on **behavioral risk, not convenience**. Planned work
keeps its auditor even when the code looks fine, its tests already pass,
the review feels redundant, or the session is running long — only an
explicit developer instruction waives the gate (see `process.mdc` "The
process is mandatory").

## Output

The verdict is **always** surfaced **inline** in the work summary (chat
or PR) so the user can decide on the spot. It is **never** written to a
file. Justified deviations are surfaced here too, not hidden (see
`human-interface/session-wrap-up-summary.mdc`, `planning/plans.mdc`).
